{
  "title": "Row Erupts in UK Parliament Over Animal Sentience Vote",
  "date": "2024-06-29",
  "source": "The Guardian",
  "story": [
    {
      "paragraph": "MPs have recently found themselves at the center of controversy after a Commons vote on animal sentience sparked widespread public concern and debate. The dispute began when an amendment to the European Union (Withdrawal) Bill, aimed at transferring the EU protocol on animal sentience into UK law, was narrowly defeated by 313 votes to 295 on November 15."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "The defeat led to accusations that Parliament had rejected the principle that animals are sentient beings. Animal rights groups, public figures, and thousands of citizens responded with protests and online petitions, voicing fears that animal welfare standards in the UK might suffer post-Brexit."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "However, several Conservative MPs, including Zac Goldsmith and Rachel Maclean, have responded to these accusations by insisting that the vote was about legal procedure rather than the fundamental recognition of animal sentience. In a tweet, Goldsmith described some media reporting on the event as 'weird and dishonest,' asserting that the government remains committed to animal welfare."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "Rachel Maclean posted a video denouncing the controversy as 'fake news' and reiterating that protections for animals as sentient beings are already part of UK law. Prime Minister Theresa May echoed these sentiments during Prime Minister's Questions, stating, 'We recognise and respect that animals are sentient beings, and should be treated accordingly.'"
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "Despite these reassurances, critics remain unconvinced. Michael Gove, the environment secretary, was drawn into the debate, with MPs such as Hugo Swire publishing correspondence from Gove online to clarify the government's stance. Swire expressed his belief that no MP disputed the reality of animal sentience."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "Prominent campaigners, including TV presenter Sue Perkins, joined animal welfare groups in criticizing MPs for rejecting the amendment. Gudrun Ravetz, senior vice-president of the British Veterinary Association, called the omission of the EU clause 'extremely concerning' and argued that it undermines the government's previous commitments to maintaining high standards after Brexit."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "Still, other MPs, such as Henry Smith, pointed out that EU law has allowed certain animal practices that UK animal rights activists oppose, suggesting that the debate is more complex than headline reports have indicated."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "The controversial amendment, co-sponsored by Green Party co-leader Caroline Lucas, sought to explicitly incorporate article 13 of Title II of the Lisbon Treaty into UK law. During the parliamentary debate, Lucas highlighted the UK’s historic leadership in embedding animal sentience into European law during its 1997 EU presidency, with the principle formally recognised in EU legislation since 1999."
    },
    {
      "paragraph": "As the government moves forward with Brexit preparations, the debate over animal sentience law looks set to continue, with many urging clearer assurances about the legal status of animal welfare in a post-EU United Kingdom."
    }
  ]
}
```

